ORGO.reg({"types":{"energy-diagrams":{"id":"energy-diagrams","title":"Read the energy diagram","exam":"Q3B","points":25,"asks":"Several reaction-coordinate diagrams are drawn. Pick the one that fits the reaction described.","know":[{"h":"A reaction-coordinate diagram plots energy (up) against the progress of the reaction (left to right): starting materials on the left, products on the right.","from":"Lecture 11/03, p. 3"},{"h":"Every <b>peak</b> is a transition state. Every <b>valley</b> between two peaks is an intermediate. The number of steps equals the number of peaks, and the number of intermediates is one fewer.","from":"Lecture 11/03, p. 3; Lecture 11/10, p. 2"},{"h":"{{Ea}} of a step is the height from the level just before the step up to its peak. For the first step that is from the starting materials. For a later step it is from the intermediate in front of it.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3; standard"},{"h":"{{dH}} is the products' level minus the starting materials' level. Products lower: <b>exothermic</b> (negative). Products higher: <b>endothermic</b> (positive). The peaks do not enter into it.","from":"standard; FA24 Exam 2, Q3B"},{"h":"The <b>rate-determining step</b> (rds) is the one whose transition state is the highest point of the whole diagram.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 2"},{"h":"<b>Concerted reactions have one hump.</b> {{SN2}}: one hump from start to product, no intermediate. {{E2}}: one hump as well.","from":"Lecture 11/03, p. 3; Lecture 11/12, p. 1"},{"h":"<b>{{SN1}} has a valley for the carbocation.</b> The first hump (ionization, ts1) is the highest point and is the rds. The second hump (attack of the nucleophile) is lower.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 2"},{"h":"Drawn in full for tert-butyl bromide and water, {{SN1}} has three steps: ionization, attack of water to give the protonated alcohol, and loss of {{H+}} to a second water. That is three peaks and two valleys (the carbocation and the protonated alcohol).","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 2"},{"h":"{{E1}} shares the first step and the carbocation with {{SN1}}. Its second step is removal of a hydrogen next to the cationic carbon: two humps, the first one higher.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 4"},{"h":"The exam question: an exothermic {{SN1}} is the diagram with two humps, a valley between them, the first hump higher, and the products below the starting materials.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q3B"},{"h":"<b>Rate comes from {{Ea}}, not from {{dH}}.</b> A very exothermic reaction can still be slow if its barrier is high.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3 to 4; standard"},{"h":"The rate law tells you the shape. If the nucleophile's concentration is in the rate law, it is in the rate-determining transition state: one hump ({{SN2}}, {{E2}}). If only the substrate is in the rate law, the slow step is ionization: two or more humps ({{SN1}}, {{E1}}).","from":"Lecture 11/03, p. 3; Lecture 11/10, p. 1; Lecture 11/12, p. 1"},{"h":"For the reverse reaction, read the same diagram from right to left: its {{Ea}} is the height from the products up to the peak.","from":"standard"}],"method":["Count the humps: one means concerted (SN2 or E2), two or more means there are intermediates (SN1 or E1).","Mark each valley between humps as an intermediate and name it (for SN1 and E1, the carbocation).","Find the highest peak: the step that climbs to it is the rate-determining step.","Compare the right-hand end with the left-hand end: lower is exothermic, higher is endothermic.","For a number, subtract levels: Ea is peak minus the level before that step, and ΔH is products minus starting materials.","Check every word of the question (exothermic, SN1, rate-determining) against the diagram you picked."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Calling a peak an intermediate. Intermediates are valleys. Peaks are transition states."},{"h":"Reading exothermic or endothermic from the height of a hump instead of from the two ends."},{"h":"Choosing a two-hump diagram for {{SN1}} without checking that the first hump is the higher one."},{"h":"Measuring {{Ea}} of a later step from the starting materials instead of from the intermediate before it."},{"h":"Deciding which reaction is faster from {{dH}}. The barrier decides."},{"h":"Stopping at the right shape and missing one word of the question, such as exothermic."}],"links":{"read":["sn2-1","sn1-e1","e2"]}}},"items":{"energy-diagrams-001":{"id":"energy-diagrams-001","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"worked","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"tert-Butyl bromide reacts with NaOH by {{E2}}. The reaction is exothermic. Which diagram represents it?","fixed":true,"choices":[{"h":"Diagram 1\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I=12,TS2=16,P=-10]]","ok":false,"why":"Exothermic, but two humps with a valley: an intermediate. This is the shape of {{SN1}} or {{E1}}, not of a concerted reaction."},{"h":"Diagram 2\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=24,I=14,TS2=20,P=8]]","ok":false,"why":"Two steps and endothermic: wrong on both counts."},{"h":"Diagram 3\n[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-10]]","ok":true,"why":"One hump (concerted, the transition state holds both the base and the substrate) and the products below the starting materials."},{"h":"Diagram 4\n[[energy:R=0,TS=22,P=8]]","ok":false,"why":"One hump is right, but the products end higher than the starting materials: endothermic."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Decide how many steps","h":"{{E2}} is concerted: the base takes the hydrogen, the π bond forms and bromide leaves all at once. One step means one hump and no valley.","ask":{"q":"How many transition states does a concerted reaction have?","a":"One."}},{"t":"Remove the wrong shapes","h":"Diagrams 1 and 2 have two humps with a valley. A valley is an intermediate, and {{E2}} has none."},{"t":"Read exothermic","h":"Exothermic means the products end below the starting materials. Diagram 3 ends at -10. Diagram 4 ends at +8.","ask":{"q":"Where do you look to decide exothermic or endothermic?","a":"Only at the two ends, left and right."}},{"t":"Check every word","h":"{{E2}}: one hump. Exothermic: ends lower. Diagram 3."}],"answer":"Diagram 3.","rule":"One hump = concerted. End below start = exothermic."}},"energy-diagrams-002":{"id":"energy-diagrams-002","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"worked","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"Use the diagram (energies in kcal/mol). What are {{Ea}} of the rate-determining step and {{dH}} of the reaction?\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I=12,TS2=16,P=-8]]","choices":[{"h":"{{Ea}} = 22, {{dH}} = -8","ok":true,"why":"The highest peak is 22 above the starting materials, and the products are 8 below them."},{"h":"{{Ea}} = 16, {{dH}} = -8","ok":false,"why":"16 is the height of the second peak, which is not the highest point, and it was not measured from the intermediate either."},{"h":"{{Ea}} = 4, {{dH}} = -8","ok":false,"why":"4 is the barrier of the second step (16 - 12). The rate-determining step is the first."},{"h":"{{Ea}} = 22, {{dH}} = -20","ok":false,"why":"-20 is the drop from the intermediate to the products. {{dH}} is measured from the starting materials."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the transition states and the intermediate","h":"Two peaks (22 and 16) and one valley (12): two steps, one intermediate.","ask":{"q":"How many intermediates?","a":"One, the valley at 12."}},{"t":"Find the rate-determining step","h":"The highest point is the first peak, 22. Step 1 is the rds.","ask":{"q":"Which step is rate-determining?","a":"The first, because its peak is the highest point."}},{"t":"Measure its barrier","h":"{{Ea}} = peak minus the level before the step = 22 - 0 = 22 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Measure the heat of reaction","h":"{{dH}} = products minus starting materials = -8 - 0 = -8 kcal/mol. Negative: exothermic.","ask":{"q":"Do the peaks matter for {{dH}}?","a":"No. Only the two ends."}},{"t":"For comparison","h":"The second step has its own barrier, 16 - 12 = 4 kcal/mol, measured from the intermediate."}],"answer":"Ea = 22 kcal/mol and ΔH = -8 kcal/mol.","rule":"Ea: up to the peak from the level before it. ΔH: end minus start."}},"energy-diagrams-003":{"id":"energy-diagrams-003","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"worked","kind":"mcq","diff":3,"src":"site","stem":"The lecture drew every step of the {{SN1}} reaction of tert-butyl bromide with water: ionization, attack of water, then loss of {{H+}}. Which diagram shows all three steps?\n[[rxn:CC(C)(C)Br + O>>CC(C)(C)O + Br]]","fixed":true,"choices":[{"h":"Diagram 1\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I=14,TS2=17,P=-6]]","ok":false,"why":"Two peaks show only ionization and attack of water. The question asks for all three steps, including the loss of {{H+}}."},{"h":"Diagram 2\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I1=14,TS2=17,I2=-2,TS3=2,P=-6|I1=carbocation,I2=protonated alcohol]]","ok":true,"why":"Three peaks, two valleys (carbocation, then protonated alcohol), and the first peak is the highest."},{"h":"Diagram 3\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=10,I1=4,TS2=14,I2=-2,TS3=22,P=-6]]","ok":false,"why":"Three steps, but the last peak is the highest. That would make the proton transfer the slow step, and the lecture's rds is ionization."},{"h":"Diagram 4\n[[energy:R=0,TS=22,P=-6]]","ok":false,"why":"One hump is a concerted reaction. {{SN1}} has a carbocation intermediate."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Count the steps","h":"Three steps need three peaks.","ask":{"q":"How many intermediates lie between three steps?","a":"Two."}},{"t":"Name the valleys","h":"After step 1: the carbocation. After step 2: the protonated alcohol, {{(CH3)3C-OH2+}}. Both have full bonds and sit in valleys."},{"t":"Place the highest peak","h":"Ionization is the rate-determining step, so the first peak (ts1) is the highest point.","ask":{"q":"Which peak is the highest?","a":"The first."}},{"t":"Choose","h":"Three peaks, two valleys, first peak highest: diagram 2."}],"answer":"Diagram 2.","rule":"Steps = peaks. The SN1 slow step is the first one."}},"energy-diagrams-004":{"id":"energy-diagrams-004","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"worked","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"In this two-step reaction, which step is rate-determining, and what is the barrier of that step?\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=12,I=6,TS2=20,P=-5]]","choices":[{"h":"Step 2, barrier 14","ok":true,"why":"The second peak is the highest point, and the step leading to it starts from the intermediate at 6."},{"h":"Step 1, barrier 12","ok":false,"why":"The first step is not automatically the slow one. Its peak, 12, is lower than the second peak."},{"h":"Step 2, barrier 25","ok":false,"why":"25 is the drop from the second peak to the products. A barrier is measured upwards, from the level before the peak."},{"h":"Step 1, barrier 6","ok":false,"why":"6 is the energy of the intermediate, which is a valley and not a barrier."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the highest point","h":"The second peak, at 20, is higher than the first, at 12.","ask":{"q":"Is the first step always the slow one?","a":"No. The slow step is the one with the highest transition state."}},{"t":"Name the rds","h":"The step that climbs to the highest point is step 2, from the intermediate to the products."},{"t":"Measure that step's barrier","h":"A step's barrier starts at the level in front of it. Step 2 starts at the intermediate, 6, so its barrier is 20 - 6 = 14.","ask":{"q":"From which level is the second step's barrier measured?","a":"From the intermediate, at 6."}},{"t":"Compare with SN1","h":"This diagram is not {{SN1}}: there the first peak is the highest."}],"answer":"Step 2, with a barrier of 14 kcal/mol.","rule":"The rate-determining step is the one with the highest peak, wherever it comes."}},"energy-diagrams-005":{"id":"energy-diagrams-005","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"site","stem":"How many transition states and how many intermediates does this reaction have?\n[[energy:R=0,TS=18,P=-10]]","choices":[{"h":"1 transition state, 0 intermediates","ok":true,"why":"A single hump: one concerted step."},{"h":"0 transition states, 1 intermediate","ok":false,"why":"The top of the hump is a transition state. An intermediate is a valley."},{"h":"1 transition state, 1 intermediate","ok":false,"why":"There is no valley between peaks, so nothing here is an intermediate."},{"h":"2 transition states, 1 intermediate","ok":false,"why":"That describes a two-hump diagram."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Count peaks","h":"One peak: one transition state."},{"t":"Count valleys between peaks","h":"None: no intermediate."},{"t":"Name the type","h":"One step with no intermediate is a concerted reaction, like {{SN2}} or {{E2}}."}],"answer":"One transition state, no intermediates.","rule":"Peaks are transition states, valleys are intermediates."}},"energy-diagrams-006":{"id":"energy-diagrams-006","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"site","stem":"Which diagram shows an <b>endothermic</b> reaction that takes place in <b>one concerted step</b>?","fixed":true,"choices":[{"h":"Diagram 1\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=20,I=10,TS2=16,P=6]]","ok":false,"why":"Endothermic, but two humps and a valley: two steps."},{"h":"Diagram 2\n[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-6]]","ok":false,"why":"One step, but the products are lower: exothermic."},{"h":"Diagram 3\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=20,I=10,TS2=16,P=-6]]","ok":false,"why":"Two steps and exothermic."},{"h":"Diagram 4\n[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=6]]","ok":true,"why":"One hump, and the products end above the starting materials."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Concerted","h":"One hump only: diagrams 2 and 4."},{"t":"Endothermic","h":"Products above the starting materials: of those two, diagram 4."},{"t":"Check","h":"Diagram 4: one hump, ends at +6."}],"answer":"Diagram 4.","rule":"Shape gives the number of steps. The ends give exothermic or endothermic."}},"energy-diagrams-007":{"id":"energy-diagrams-007","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":1,"src":"site","stem":"What is {{dH}} for this reaction (energies in kcal/mol)?\n[[energy:R=0,TS=18,P=-10]]","choices":[{"h":"-10 kcal/mol","ok":true,"why":"The products are 10 below the starting materials."},{"h":"+18 kcal/mol","ok":false,"why":"18 is {{Ea}}, the height of the barrier."},{"h":"-28 kcal/mol","ok":false,"why":"28 is the drop from the transition state to the products, which is the barrier of the reverse reaction."},{"h":"+10 kcal/mol","ok":false,"why":"The sign is wrong: the products are lower, so heat is given out."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Use only the ends","h":"Starting materials at 0, products at -10."},{"t":"Subtract","h":"{{dH}} = -10 - 0 = -10 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Name it","h":"Negative: exothermic. The 18 is {{Ea}}."}],"answer":"-10 kcal/mol (exothermic).","rule":"ΔH = products minus starting materials."}},"energy-diagrams-008":{"id":"energy-diagrams-008","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"What is {{Ea}} of the <b>second</b> step (energies in kcal/mol)?\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=20,I=11,TS2=16,P=-4]]","choices":[{"h":"5","ok":true,"why":"From the intermediate at 11 up to the second peak at 16."},{"h":"16","ok":false,"why":"Measured from the starting materials. The second step begins at the intermediate."},{"h":"20","ok":false,"why":"That is the barrier of the first step."},{"h":"9","ok":false,"why":"9 is the drop from the first peak to the intermediate, not a barrier."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Find the second step","h":"From the intermediate (11) over the second peak (16) to the products."},{"t":"Measure from the level before it","h":"16 - 11 = 5 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Sanity check","h":"The second step is fast: its barrier is much smaller than the first step's 20."}],"answer":"5 kcal/mol.","rule":"A step's Ea is measured from the valley in front of it."}},"energy-diagrams-009":{"id":"energy-diagrams-009","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"tert-Butyl bromide gives 2-methylpropene by {{E1}} when heated in water. Which diagram fits?\n[[rxn:CC(C)(C)Br>H2O;heat>CC(C)=C]]","fixed":true,"choices":[{"h":"Diagram 1\n[[energy:R=0,TS=22,P=3]]","ok":false,"why":"One hump is {{E2}}, the concerted elimination with a strong base. {{E1}} goes through a carbocation."},{"h":"Diagram 2\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=14,I=8,TS2=22,P=3]]","ok":false,"why":"Two steps, but the second peak is the highest. In {{E1}} the slow step is the ionization, which comes first."},{"h":"Diagram 3\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I=14,TS2=17,P=3|I=carbocation]]","ok":true,"why":"Two humps with the carbocation between them, and ionization, the first hump, is the highest point."},{"h":"Diagram 4\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I1=14,TS2=17,I2=8,TS3=12,P=3]]","ok":false,"why":"Three steps. {{E1}} has two: ionization, then loss of the proton directly from the carbocation."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"List the steps of E1","h":"Step 1: the C-Br bond ionizes to the carbocation (slow). Step 2: water removes a hydrogen from the carbon next to the cationic carbon and the C=C forms."},{"t":"Translate","h":"Two steps: two peaks and one valley, the carbocation. The slow first step has the higher peak."},{"t":"Choose","h":"Diagram 3."}],"answer":"Diagram 3.","rule":"E1 has the same first step and the same intermediate as SN1."}},"energy-diagrams-010":{"id":"energy-diagrams-010","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"This is the diagram for tert-butyl bromide reacting with water. What sits in the valley marked X?\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,X=14,TS2=17,P=-6]]","choices":[{"h":"The tert-butyl carbocation, [[mol:C[C+](C)C]]","ok":true,"why":"The intermediate formed by ionization, as in the lecture diagram: carbocation, {{Br-}} and {{H2O}} in the valley."},{"h":"The transition state [{{(CH3)3C}}<sup>δ+</sup>···Br<sup>δ-</sup>]‡","ok":false,"why":"A transition state is a peak. This species is the first peak, not the valley."},{"h":"tert-Butyl alcohol","ok":false,"why":"That is the product, at the right-hand end."},{"h":"A carbon with five bonds, to three {{CH3}}, Br and {{OH2}}","ok":false,"why":"That is the idea of an {{SN2}} transition state, which is neither a valley nor possible at a tertiary carbon."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Valley or peak","h":"X is a valley between two peaks, so it is an intermediate with full bonds."},{"t":"What step 1 makes","h":"The C-Br bond breaks: the tert-butyl carbocation and bromide."},{"t":"Answer","h":"The carbocation (with {{Br-}} and water alongside)."}],"answer":"The tert-butyl carbocation.","rule":"Valley = intermediate. In SN1 and E1 it is the carbocation."}},"energy-diagrams-011":{"id":"energy-diagrams-011","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"site","stem":"What is {{Ea}} for the <b>reverse</b> reaction, products going back to starting materials (energies in kcal/mol)?\n[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-8]]","choices":[{"h":"28","ok":true,"why":"From -8 up to 20."},{"h":"20","ok":false,"why":"That is the forward barrier, measured from the starting materials."},{"h":"12","ok":false,"why":"20 - 8: the sign of the product level was dropped. The products are at -8, so the gap is 20 + 8."},{"h":"8","ok":false,"why":"8 is the size of {{dH}}, not a barrier."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read from right to left","h":"The reverse reaction starts at the products, -8."},{"t":"Climb to the same peak","h":"20 - (-8) = 28 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Connect","h":"The reverse barrier of an exothermic reaction is the forward barrier plus the heat given out: 20 + 8."}],"answer":"28 kcal/mol.","rule":"Both directions share one transition state. Each barrier is measured from its own starting level."}},"energy-diagrams-012":{"id":"energy-diagrams-012","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":3,"src":"site","stem":"Two reactions are run at the same temperature. Which is faster, and why?\nReaction A: [[energy:R=0,TS=25,P=-20]]\nReaction B: [[energy:R=0,TS=15,P=-3]]","choices":[{"h":"B, because its {{Ea}} is smaller","ok":true,"why":"15 against 25. Fewer molecules have enough energy to cross the higher barrier of A."},{"h":"A, because it is more exothermic","ok":false,"why":"How much heat is released does not set the rate. A has the higher barrier and is slower."},{"h":"A, because its transition state is higher in energy","ok":false,"why":"A higher transition state means a larger barrier and a slower reaction."},{"h":"They are equally fast, because both are exothermic and one step","ok":false,"why":"Same shape, different barriers. The rates differ."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the barriers","h":"A: 25. B: 15."},{"t":"Read the heats","h":"A: -20. B: -3. A gives out more heat."},{"t":"Decide what controls rate","h":"The rate depends on the barrier, {{Ea}}. The lower barrier wins, whatever the heat of reaction."}],"answer":"B, because its Ea is smaller.","rule":"Rate comes from Ea. ΔH says how far downhill, not how fast."},"fixed":true},"energy-diagrams-013":{"id":"energy-diagrams-013","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":3,"src":"site","stem":"Here is the full diagram for tert-butyl bromide and water. How many intermediates are there, and in which transition state is the C-Br bond partly broken?\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I1=14,TS2=17,I2=-2,TS3=2,P=-6]]","choices":[{"h":"2 intermediates, TS1","ok":true,"why":"The valleys are the carbocation and the protonated alcohol. Bromide leaves in the first step."},{"h":"1 intermediate, TS1","ok":false,"why":"The protonated alcohol, {{(CH3)3C-OH2+}}, is an intermediate too. It is the second valley."},{"h":"3 intermediates, TS1","ok":false,"why":"The three peaks were counted. Peaks are transition states."},{"h":"2 intermediates, TS2","ok":false,"why":"By TS2 the bromide has already gone. TS2 is water bonding to the carbocation."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Count the valleys between peaks","h":"Two: the carbocation and the protonated alcohol."},{"t":"Match steps to peaks","h":"TS1: ionization, C-Br breaking. TS2: water attacking the carbocation, C-O forming. TS3: a second water removing {{H+}}, O-H breaking."},{"t":"Answer","h":"Two intermediates, and the C-Br bond is partly broken in TS1."}],"answer":"Two intermediates. TS1.","rule":"Each peak belongs to one step: know which bond changes in each."}},"energy-diagrams-014":{"id":"energy-diagrams-014","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"practice","kind":"mcq","diff":3,"src":"site","stem":"For an exothermic substitution reaction of R-Br with a nucleophile Nu, these rates were measured. Which diagram fits?<table><tr><th>trial</th><th>[R-Br]</th><th>[Nu]</th><th>relative rate</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr></table>","fixed":true,"choices":[{"h":"Diagram 1\n[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-8]]","ok":false,"why":"One hump is {{SN2}}, where rate = k[R-Br][Nu]. The table shows the rate does not depend on [Nu]."},{"h":"Diagram 2\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=14,I=8,TS2=22,P=-8]]","ok":false,"why":"Two steps, but the second peak is highest. If the step with the nucleophile were slow, [Nu] would be in the rate law."},{"h":"Diagram 3\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I=12,TS2=16,P=-8]]","ok":true,"why":"Rate = k[R-Br] means {{SN1}}: two humps, the first one highest, and the products lower for an exothermic reaction."},{"h":"Diagram 4\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=24,I=14,TS2=20,P=8]]","ok":false,"why":"The right shape for {{SN1}}, but the products end higher: endothermic."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Get the rate law from the table","h":"Doubling [R-Br] doubles the rate. Doubling [Nu] changes nothing. Rate = k[R-Br]: unimolecular."},{"t":"Name the mechanism","h":"The nucleophile is not in the slow step, so the substrate ionizes first: {{SN1}}."},{"t":"Translate to a diagram","h":"A carbocation valley between two humps, the first hump higher."},{"t":"Apply exothermic","h":"Products below the starting materials: diagram 3."}],"answer":"Diagram 3.","rule":"Rate law first, then shape, then the ends."}},"exam-q3b-diagram":{"id":"exam-q3b-diagram","type":"energy-diagrams","role":"exam","kind":"mcq","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q3B","fixed":true,"stem":"Which reaction coordinate diagram represents an exothermic {{SN1}} reaction?","choices":[{"h":"Diagram 1\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=22,I=12,TS2=16,P=-8]]","ok":true,"why":"Two humps with a valley between them (the carbocation), the first hump the higher one (ionization is rate-determining), and the products below the reactants (exothermic)."},{"h":"Diagram 2\n[[energy:R=0,TS1=24,I=14,TS2=20,P=8]]","ok":false,"why":"The shape is right for two steps, but the products end higher than the reactants: this is an endothermic reaction. The word exothermic was overlooked."},{"h":"Diagram 3\n[[energy:R=0,TS=20,P=-8]]","ok":false,"why":"Exothermic, but only one hump and no intermediate: a concerted reaction such as SN2 or E2. SN1 must show the carbocation as a valley."},{"h":"Diagram 4\n[[energy:R=0,TS=22,P=8]]","ok":false,"why":"One hump means one concerted step, not SN1, and the products end higher than the reactants, so it is endothermic as well."}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Count the steps","h":"SN1 has an intermediate, the carbocation. An intermediate is a valley between two humps. One hump means a concerted reaction (SN2, E2). That removes diagrams 3 and 4."},{"t":"Find the slow step","h":"The first step, ionization, is the rate-determining step: the first hump is the highest point of the diagram."},{"t":"Read exothermic","h":"Exothermic means the products are lower in energy than the reactants. Diagram 1 ends below its start. Diagram 2 ends above it."}],"answer":"Diagram 1","rule":"Humps = steps, valleys = intermediates, end below start = exothermic."}}},"mols":{"CC(C)(C)Br":{"w":64,"h":68,"p":[[0,"M 7.8,49.1 L 31.7,35.3",0],[1,"M 31.7,35.3 L 55.5,21.5",0],[2,"M 31.7,35.3 L 45.4,59.2",0],[3,"M 31.7,35.3 L 20.5,15.9",0]],"t":[[4,8.7,17,11,"B"],[4,16.3,17,11,"r"]],"a":[[7.8,49.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,35.3,"C",0,0,0,0],[55.5,21.5,"C",0,0,3,0],[45.4,59.2,"C",0,0,3,0],[17.9,11.5,"Br",0,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1]]},"O":{"w":22,"h":17,"p":[],"t":[[0,3,10,10,"H"],[0,9.4,14,6,"2"],[0,12.3,10,10,"O"]],"a":[[15.3,5,"O",0,2,2,0]],"b":[]},"CC(C)(C)O":{"w":64,"h":68,"p":[[0,"M 8.0,49.1 L 31.8,35.3",0],[1,"M 31.8,35.3 L 55.7,21.5",0],[2,"M 31.8,35.3 L 45.6,59.2",0],[3,"M 31.8,35.3 L 21.8,17.9",0]],"t":[[4,7.7,17,11,"H"],[4,14.7,17,11,"O"]],"a":[[8,49.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.8,35.3,"C",0,0,0,0],[55.7,21.5,"C",0,0,3,0],[45.6,59.2,"C",0,0,3,0],[18.1,11.5,"O",0,2,1,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1]]},"Br":{"w":23,"h":11,"p":[],"t":[[0,3.2,9.3,10,"H"],[0,10.1,9.3,10,"B"],[0,16.5,9.3,10,"r"]],"a":[[17.9,4.3,"Br",0,3,1,0]],"b":[]},"CC(C)=C":{"w":64,"h":55,"p":[[0,"M 8.0,47.6 L 31.7,33.9",0],[1,"M 31.7,33.9 L 55.4,47.6",0],[2,"M 33.7,35.1 L 33.7,6.6",0],[2,"M 29.6,35.1 L 29.6,6.6",0],[-1,"M 30.5,34.6 L 31.7,33.9 L 32.9,34.6",0]],"t":[],"a":[[8,47.6,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,33.9,"C",0,0,0,0],[55.4,47.6,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,6.6,"C",0,0,2,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,2]]},"C[C+](C)C":{"w":64,"h":55,"p":[[0,"M 8.0,47.6 L 27.5,36.3",0],[1,"M 35.9,36.3 L 55.4,47.6",0],[2,"M 31.7,29.8 L 31.7,6.6",0]],"t":[[1,28.4,39.4,10,"C"],[1,35.9,35,7,"+"]],"a":[[8,47.6,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,33.9,"C",1,0,0,0],[55.4,47.6,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,6.6,"C",0,0,3,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1]]}}});
